  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PY_LOOP_AND_IF
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PY_LOOP_AND_IF
  language: 1
  conceptTitle: Loops and Conditions Together
  symbol: for+if
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PY_10_MIXED
  topics:
  - Mixed
  definition: Real programs often combine ideas. Putting an if inside a loop lets
    a program check every item and act only on the ones that match.
  howItWorks: The for line goes through the items one by one. The if line is indented
    inside the loop, so it is checked for every item. The print is indented again,
    so it only runs when the condition is True. The % operator gives the remainder
    of a division, which is handy for testing numbers.
  exampleCode: "nums = [5, 8, 11, 12]\nfor n in nums:\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n       
    print(n)"
  exampleExplanation: n % 2 is the remainder after dividing by 2. For 5 and 11 the
    remainder is 1, so they are printed. For 8 and 12 the remainder is 0, so the
    condition is False and nothing is printed. This loop prints the odd numbers 5
    and 11.
  tip: Both the for line and the if line need a colon at the end.
